#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134636} :activity/id #uuid "600046de-0665-50c3-a999-1a23cc41313b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135034 :tutorial/content "The standard inner product of $\\Bbb R^n,$ i.e. the dot product, does not meet the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFor example, if $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is a valid inner product on $\\Bbb C^n,$ then for any vector $\\mathbf a \\in \\Bbb C^n,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathbf a, \\, \\mathbf a\\rangle \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nBut for many vectors in $\\Bbb C^n,$ such as $[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ in $\\Bbb C^2,$ the dot product fails to satisfy this criterion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & ={\\text{i}}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThankfully, there is an elegant way to generalize the dot product to satisfy the desired criterion on $\\Bbb C^n.$ We can just take the complex conjugate of each element in the right vector before carrying out the dot product.\n\nMore formally, for two vectors $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in \\mathbb C^n$ given by\n\n![](images/26/26b805ac1354eab1901a0874f66882bc1ec07dc3351c95ad2cf9b16aaf59d2fb.svg)\n\nwe define the **standard Hermitian inner product** as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{i}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{y_i}$ is the complex conjugate of $y_i.$\n\nUsing the standard Hermitian inner product instead of the dot product, our desired criterion is satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]⟩ & =\\text{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,\\text{i}\\,}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,0\\,} \\\\  & =\\text{i}\\cdot (-\\text{i})+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ge 0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be verified that the Hermitian inner product satisfies *all* the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFurthermore, since the complex conjugate leaves real numbers unchanged, the Hermitian inner product simplifies to the dot product on $\\Bbb R^n.$ In other words, the dot product is just a special case of the Hermitian inner product!" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca70c748-2628-5d2d-b0dc-ab7a0675355a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3605 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5959b5ca-9405-5f0f-b4d5-5a7a8e442cfc" :step/math-academy-id 20193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133574 :question/id #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5545"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048861}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411853}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "1f2f6fb1-f106-52c7-b55a-201253d4ed07" :step/math-academy-id 20194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133575 :question/id #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" :question/math-academy-id "e-5547"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360785}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "f393944b-d78a-5d8a-aa1a-c7c5133f9717" :step/math-academy-id 20196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135100 :tutorial/content "A Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is **linear in the first argument.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is **antilinear in the second argument.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has **conjugate symmetry.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is **positive-definite.** For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44cf93d3-a069-54ee-bfbf-93d36fd338cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5088 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Inner Product Over the Complex Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f16cfe03-2cd2-5567-81c0-5f200f0b4b5f" :step/math-academy-id 31867 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133865 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133681 :question/id #uuid "8d5f83f0-dfb9-5437-a0e5-f33f68a59cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8578"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360771}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "418336f6-44aa-5629-a383-cc4e0867f9c9" :step/math-academy-id 31868}] :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133984}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445245} :activity/id #uuid "871d2b00-341d-5ec3-92a3-8fd5aa7eca28" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b1189c0-3251-5c7c-817b-7a39c0cfb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358541} {:answer/id #uuid "91265db6-dce7-5eec-9519-3d3a281089d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359026} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b75f4a-48bb-5311-bdcf-94fee8436a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359360} {:answer/id #uuid "da05ae5f-5078-577d-94a3-569381365c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359660} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0dd3c8-34b2-590d-8552-ba6dcd34cd6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359973} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a26644-5b9a-583b-8d85-eae43b25ea9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2fac0a4e-d3e8-507c-b2ff-dcb288cf0e43" :question/math-academy-id "q-97258" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n\nII. $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n\nIII. $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "042f271a-62ae-5a4a-884d-ff79b9db9024" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445244}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e841129-5370-59a4-a247-981a63a55e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357872} {:answer/id #uuid "0f6fe134-1090-5f97-9ea8-128ddfc3b774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357876} {:answer/id #uuid "c020effb-ac1b-5c1a-b136-a3f990641cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359435} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7d8484-175b-5e8d-bc3b-f162579d8e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359541} {:answer/id #uuid "e819a706-cc55-53d4-ad1b-913fba1a9b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359541} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14982e8-8062-5f22-9abd-32b12535e617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "34140471-4f5c-5a2d-9e46-e76840a3d73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97193" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩$ if  \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=-1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩ & =⟨a,(4-\\text{i})b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c\\text{ }⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1-\\text{i}})+5 \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (-1+\\text{i})+5 \\\\  & =-5+3\\text{i}+5 \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22a3d38c-45ed-5261-9a9f-bdb8efdf26a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445241}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e483cc2-b46f-5512-a9a7-0f10ea8a953d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269394} {:answer/id #uuid "4288a7d7-b334-57bd-981d-9101054485a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269869} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf5a7d9-2cd0-5edb-8159-5892ad0bc218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269954} {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270242} {:answer/id #uuid "cf364781-4d57-5ea3-a715-fb67ec1553ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186270242} :answer-field/id #uuid "672798eb-6f95-52b3-882f-b5bf772f3083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "396a82c3-ba69-5d62-8d39-94fffd303517" :question/math-academy-id "q-141160" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\nII. $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n\nIII. $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4ce6b6ce-a0a5-5ded-b78a-9436ef265d55"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445244 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fdcdad3-e1b1-5a7a-b43d-f503abcdcd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358030} {:answer/id #uuid "47bb2880-6105-5884-a910-6ff3fb40c799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358369} {:answer/id #uuid "b33621c1-471e-5f87-b4dd-e6a4f7c2271e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359313} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b5ffce-32b5-5567-8de2-f6e5178d0127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359611} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2853e0-f5e9-5edf-9276-d50fe0a40a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358030} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef80d06-a51d-581a-a204-f6dcc0201009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "354bca31-ea8a-5e1d-a896-d3e34e677d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-97296" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\nII. $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\nIII. There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c0e9081f-8d78-5a6a-b192-5ac752a618f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cda7ef33-fa7f-5bfa-b9a6-e5261e06d70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382622} {:answer/id #uuid "944aaef0-c92b-5460-b4bf-078c629fb5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382623} {:answer/id #uuid "0a96dcaf-f1c7-527a-b08a-1d68a986f634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382624} {:answer/id #uuid "27ad374f-3361-5475-8073-b762bb17dd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382625} {:answer/id #uuid "14945f42-7413-59f1-969e-cad3bb3baa75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382625} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbcb91a3-9ab8-5494-a94e-36070be9094c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f2e5445-afeb-5118-844a-129faadbb234" :question/math-academy-id "q-96821" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=1+\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,b⟩-⟨a,(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{1+\\text{i}}-(1-3\\text{i})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}-2+6\\text{i} \\\\  & =-1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff45ad40-043f-5835-bb86-3d1e620fd852" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445242}}] :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445240}]}
